With the automatic JSX runtime (the default in Vite, Next.js and TypeScript's "jsx": "react-jsx"), this:
<Button kind="primary" onClick={save}>Save</Button>
compiles to roughly:
import { jsx as _jsx } from "react/jsx-runtime";
_jsx(Button, { kind: "primary", onClick: save, children: "Save" });
The result is an ordinary object (el.type === Button, el.props.kind === "primary"). Nothing has rendered yet; Button has not even been called. The element only records which component to call and with which props. React decides later whether to call it at all.

The tree is a linked list (child, sibling, return), so React can walk it with a plain loop instead of recursion. That is what makes pausing possible: the loop can stop after any fiber and resume later from a saved pointer.
// Very simplified shape of the loop
function workLoopConcurrent() {
while (workInProgress !== null && !shouldYield()) {
performUnitOfWork(workInProgress);
}
}
function performUnitOfWork(fiber) {
const next = beginWork(fiber); // call the component, reconcile its children
if (next === null) completeUnitOfWork(fiber); // no child: complete, go to sibling or parent
else workInProgress = next;
}
beginWork goes down: it calls the component (or bails out), then reconciles the returned children into child fibers.
completeWork goes back up: for host fibers it creates or prepares DOM nodes (not yet attached) and bubbles flags upward so the commit knows where to look.
shouldYield asks the Scheduler whether the current time slice is used up. Synchronous updates use a loop without this check; transitions use the one above. The slice is only a few milliseconds long, so the browser gets regular chances to handle input.
When the root completes, React swaps the pointer: the workInProgress tree becomes current. That swap happens during the commit, which is why a half-finished render is never visible.
Trick: the React DevTools "Components" tab shows the fiber tree (host and component fibers), not the DOM. When a DevTools tree and the Elements panel disagree about nesting, the fiber tree is what React reconciles.

function Page({ isAdmin }) {
return isAdmin ? <Profile role="admin" /> : <Profile role="user" />;
}
Both branches produce a Profile at the same position. React sees the same type, so it keeps the state of Profile and only changes the role prop. This surprises people who expect "a different branch" to mean "a fresh component".

function List({ items }) {
// A new function object on every render of List
function Row({ item }) {
const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
return <li onClick={() => setOpen(!open)}>{item.name} {open && "▾"}</li>;
}
return <ul>{items.map(i => <Row key={i.id} item={i} />)}</ul>;
}
Every render of List creates a new Row function. The element type is compared by reference, so Row from this render is a different type from Row last render. Every row is unmounted and remounted on every render: state resets, focus is lost, effects re-run, and it is slow. Define components at module level. The react-hooks/static-components lint rule in eslint-plugin-react-hooks v6 reports this pattern.
Common pitfall: conditional wrappers change the tree shape. {loading ? <Spinner /> : null}<Form /> keeps Form in slot 2 either way, but {loading ? <div><Form /></div> : <Form />} moves Form to a different depth and resets its state every time loading flips.

React skips work in these cases:
Case · What React does
setState(x) where Object.is(x, current) · Usually skips the update. React may still call the component once before it can tell the result is the same, but it does not go on into the children or commit.
Fiber has no pending work and its props object is the same reference as last time · Reuses the fiber and skips calling it; checks childLanes to decide whether to go deeper.
memo(Component) and every prop is Object.is equal · Skips the render (unless its own state or a read context changed).
Subtree has no pending lanes · Whole subtree reused from current.
The second row is the basis of the children-as-props pattern:
function ScrollTracker({ children }) {
const [y, setY] = useState(0);
useScrollListener(setY); // calls setY on every scroll
return <div data-y={y}>{children}</div>;
}
// In the parent:
<ScrollTracker><ExpensiveDashboard /></ScrollTracker>
<ExpensiveDashboard /> was created by the parent, not by ScrollTracker. When ScrollTracker re-renders on every scroll, children is the same element object as before, so React bails out of ExpensiveDashboard without any memo. Moving state down, or lifting content up into children, often beats memoisation.

Sometimes state must follow a prop. The effect version renders twice with a stale frame in between. React supports setting state during render for the component that is rendering, guarded by a comparison:
function Selection({ items }) {
const [prevItems, setPrevItems] = useState(items);
const [selected, setSelected] = useState(null);
if (items !== prevItems) { // allowed: updates this component during its own render
setPrevItems(items);
setSelected(null);
}
// ...
}
React throws away the JSX of the first pass and re-runs the component immediately, before children render. Prefer a key reset or deriving the value; use this only when those do not fit.
Common pitfall: "I wrapped the child in memo and it still renders." Check, in order: a new object/array/function prop created in the parent's render; a context the child reads; the child's own state; children passed as JSX (a new element object every render, so memo never matches).

const [n, setN] = useState(0);
function handleClick() {
setN(n + 1); setN(n + 1); setN(n + 1);
console.log(n); // still 0
}
One click shows 1, not 3. n is a constant for this render; each call queues "replace with 0 + 1". An updater function reads the queued value instead:
setN(prev => prev + 1) three times gives 3. The console.log still prints 0. The new value exists only in the next render.

React groups several state updates into a single render. Before React 18 this happened only inside React event handlers; updates in setTimeout, promises or native listeners each caused their own render. With createRoot (React 18+), batching is automatic everywhere:
fetch("/api/user").then(r => r.json()).then(user => {
setUser(user);
setLoading(false); // one render in React 18+, two in React 17
});
Apps still on the legacy ReactDOM.render root kept the old behaviour in React 18; React 19 removed ReactDOM.render, so every React 19 app batches automatically.

Rarely you need the DOM to be updated now, for example to scroll to a newly added item:
import { flushSync } from "react-dom";
function addMessage(msg) {
flushSync(() => setMessages(m => [...m, msg]));
listRef.current.lastElementChild.scrollIntoView();
}
flushSync forces a synchronous render and commit. It is expensive and it defeats concurrent features for that update, so keep it for DOM-measurement cases.
Trick: batching also explains why a click handler that calls three different setX functions produces one entry, not three, in the Profiler.

React sets ref.current to the DOM node in the commit and back to null when it is removed. For logic that must run when a node appears or goes away, use a callback ref. React 19 lets it return a cleanup function:
<div
ref={node => {
const observer = new ResizeObserver(([entry]) => setWidth(entry.contentRect.width));
observer.observe(node);
return () => observer.disconnect(); // React 19: called on detach
}}
/>
With a cleanup returned, React calls the cleanup on detach instead of calling the callback with null. Because the arrow function above is a new function every render, React detaches and re-attaches it on every render; wrap it in useCallback (or let the React Compiler memoise it) if that is costly.

Function components now receive ref as a normal prop. forwardRef is no longer needed and the React team plans to deprecate it:
function TextField({ label, ref, ...rest }) {
return (
<label>
{label}
<input ref={ref} {...rest} />
</label>
);
}
<TextField label="Email" ref={emailRef} />
To expose a small imperative API instead of the raw node, combine the ref prop with useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({ play, pause }), []).
Trick: keep imperative handles small and verb-shaped (focus, play, scrollToRow). Exposing the whole DOM node lets every parent reach in and couple itself to your markup.

function App({ flag }) {
return flag ? <Counter label="A" /> : <Counter label="B" />;
}
Click Counter three times while flag is true, then flip flag. Does the count reset? Now change the code so that it does.
Solution
It does not reset. Both branches render Counter at the same position with the same type, so React keeps the fiber and its state and only updates label. To reset, give the branches different keys:
return flag ? <Counter key="A" label="A" /> : <Counter key="B" label="B" />;

function Parent() {
const [n, setN] = useState(0);
return (
<>
<button onClick={() => setN(n + 1)}>+</button>
<Child />
<MemoChild style={{ color: "red" }} />
</>
);
}
const MemoChild = memo(function MemoChild({ style }) { return <p style={style}>hi</p>; });
After one click, which components render?
Solution
Parent, Child and MemoChild all render. Child renders because its parent rendered. MemoChild renders because style is a new object literal on every render, so the Object.is comparison fails. Hoist the object to module scope (or useMemo it, or let the React Compiler do it) and MemoChild bails out.

This effect sends two "join" messages in development. Fix it without disabling StrictMode.
useEffect(() => {
chat.send({ type: "join", roomId });
}, [roomId]);
Solution
Pair the setup with a cleanup so that setup → cleanup → setup leaves the server in the right state:
useEffect(() => {
chat.send({ type: "join", roomId });
return () => chat.send({ type: "leave", roomId });
}, [roomId]);
If the protocol cannot express "leave", the join belongs in the event that caused it (for example the click that opened the room), not in an effect.

What does one click show and log? setN(n + 5); setN(prev => prev + 1); setTimeout(() => console.log(n), 0);
Solution
The button shows n + 6 (the queue is "replace with n + 5", then "add 1"). The log prints the old n: the timeout's closure captured this render's constant.
